course: dictionary
lesson: 13467
title: Hurried and its forms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hurry means to move or do something fast because there is not much time. Hurried is the past form of hurry, so it tells you about something that already happened: «Sam hurried to school because he was late.» Here hurried means moved fast.
explain: Hurried can also describe a thing. «She ate a hurried breakfast.» A hurried breakfast is one eaten quickly, with no time to sit and enjoy it. Clues such as late, rush or not much time tell you the word is about speed.
explain: Sometimes hurried also suggests carelessness, because rushing can cause mistakes. «The hurried note had two spelling mistakes.» Look at the words around hurried to decide whether it means quick, careless, or both.
fact: Hurried means done quickly because there is not much time.
fact: Hurried is also the past form of hurry: «He hurried home.»
fact: «Hurried footsteps» are fast steps, often from someone who is short of time.
fact: Hurried can suggest carelessness, as in «a hurried note».
fact: Hurry, hurries, hurried and hurrying are all forms of the same verb.
exercise: «Mia hurried to catch the bus.» What does hurried mean here? | Sat down slowly | Sang happily | *Moved fast, in a rush | Lost her way
exercise: «She had a hurried breakfast of toast.» What does hurried mean? | Eaten slowly, with time to enjoy it | Cooked for a long time | Not eaten at all | *Eaten quickly, with no time to spare
exercise: «Tom hurries to school, but yesterday he hurried.» Find and copy the past form of hurry. | yesterday | *hurried | Tom | school
test: «The hurried note had two spelling mistakes.» What does hurried suggest here? | Written very neatly | Lost in the post | *Done quickly and perhaps carelessly | Very long and detailed
test: «Ben hurries to finish his homework before dinner.» Which word is another form of hurry? | Before | Dinner | *Hurries | Homework
exam: In «She left a hurried goodbye and ran for the train», which word describes the goodbye? | train | *hurried | ran | she
